I"�<p>To prove a digital skill, connect a specific capability to observable work, assessment and credible provenance. A list of software names or a certificate image is not sufficient evidence of competence.</p>

<h2 id="use-a-capabilityartifactassessment-pattern">Use a capability–artifact–assessment pattern</h2>

<p>Name the capability in concrete terms, show an appropriate artifact and explain how it was assessed. For collaboration, a project history may be relevant; for data analysis, a documented dataset transformation and interpretation may be more useful than a tool logo.</p>

<p><img class="img-fluid r-16" src="/img/blog/digital-skills-toolkit.png" alt="Different forms of digital toolkit" style="display: block; margin: 0 auto;" /></p>

<h2 id="keep-the-evidence-proportionate">Keep the evidence proportionate</h2>

<p>Use an authorized portfolio, protected assessment reference or concise description rather than publishing confidential work. Explain what you contributed and what the assessment demonstrates. Do not imply that completing a course proves every related job skill.</p>

<p><img class="img-fluid r-16" src="/img/blog/validating-digital-skills.png" alt="Different ways to validate digital skills" style="display: block; margin: 0 auto;" /></p>

<h2 id="for-institutions-review-the-mapping">For institutions: review the mapping</h2>

<p>Academic teams should review the relationship between assessed outcomes and taxonomy terms. <a href="/skills-taxonomy-mapping">Skills taxonomy mapping</a> keeps institutional terminology authoritative while relating it to reference vocabularies such as ESCO or O*NET.</p>

<p><img class="img-fluid r-16" src="/img/blog/Real-Life-Real-Proof-Meet-Chris-and-Sam.png" alt="through validation Chris got the call back" style="display: block; margin: 0 auto;" /></p>

<h2 id="use-credentials-to-carry-the-assessed-claim">Use credentials to carry the assessed claim</h2>

<p>An <a href="/blog/why-institutions-should-embrace-open-badges-3-0-standards">Open Badges achievement record</a> can describe issuer, criteria and evidence when implemented appropriately. Check what was awarded and whether proof/status resources can be inspected.</p>

<p><img class="img-fluid r-16" src="/img/blog/upskilling-university-staff-globally.png" alt="Benefits of upskilling university staff digitally" style="display: block; margin: 0 auto;" /></p>

<h2 id="build-a-record-without-overstating-it">Build a record without overstating it</h2>

<p>A <a href="/comprehensive-learner-record">Comprehensive Learner Record</a> can connect achievements and their provenance. It is a way to organize evidence, not an independent identity or competency examination.</p>

<h2 id="relate-skills-to-opportunities-carefully">Relate skills to opportunities carefully</h2>

<p>Use <a href="/workforce-intelligence">workforce intelligence</a> to investigate occupational requirements and market signals. A matched term is a prompt for review, not proof of job readiness or a promise of employment. Ask career services to help explain gaps and next learning steps.</p>

<h2 id="build-a-skills-portfolio">Build a skills portfolio</h2>

<p>A learner could present an assessed spreadsheet model, a short explanation of data quality decisions and an institution-issued achievement with criteria.</p>

<h2 id="plan-your-next-step">Plan your next step</h2>

<p>Discuss the actual award, evidence and implementation requirements with your institution. <a href="https://info.certifyme.online/request-demo">Request an institutional demo</a>.</p>

